# CI framework internals

The `_CI/` directory is the vendored [Invoke](https://www.pyinvoke.org/)-based workflow framework behind `./workflow.cmd`. This page is the lookup reference for its building blocks — for the task catalog see [Invoke task catalog](invoke-tasks.md), for the design rationale see [The _CI tasks architecture](../explanation/the-ci-tasks-architecture.md).

## Shared utilities (`_CI/tasks/shared.py`)

| Utility | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `execute(ctx, cmd)` | Run a shell command; raise `SystemExit(1)` on failure |
| `@run(cmd)` | Decorator — replace function body with a shell command |
| `@logged(name)` | Decorator — print pass/fail status after execution |
| `run_steps(*fns)` | Run all steps, accumulate failures, raise once at the end |

## Configuration (`_CI/tasks/configuration.py`)

Centralized constants shared across task modules:

| Constant | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `PATHS` | Standard directories targeted by linters/formatters: `src/ _CI/tasks/ tests/` |
| `CODE_FILES`, `SRC_FILES`, `PYPROJECT_FILES` | Path filters of the per-file preflight steps: which staged files each step accepts. `PYPROJECT_FILES` is why the commit hook runs `lint.pyproject` only when `pyproject.toml` is staged |
| `PROJECT_NAME` | The package slug, used for the SBOM path and the build-environment component |
| `SECURITY_OVERRIDE_ENV` | Environment variable name for security audit overrides |
| `SECURITY_OVERRIDES_FILE` | `.security-overrides`, the committed suppression list |
| `IGNORE_PATTERN` | Regex for parsing vulnerability IDs with optional expiry dates |
| `MAX_SUPPRESSION_DAYS` | How far out an expiry can be before `secure.audit` remarks that it is not really one |
| `AUDIT_REQUIREMENTS`, `AUDIT_ATTEMPTS`, `AUDIT_VERDICTS` | Where `secure.audit` writes the requirement files it derives from `uv.lock`, how many times it retries a pip-audit run that reported nothing, and the phrases that count as a report |
| `UV_LOCK`, `VENDOR_TXT`, `VENDOR_DIR` | The lockfile and the vendored tree's manifest and directory, read by the audit and the SBOM |
| `SBOM_FILE` | `src/<slug>/sbom.cdx.json`, inside the package so `uv build` ships it |
| `IMAGE_NAME` | Container image name for the deps cache |
| `DOCKERFILE_DEPS` | Dockerfile for the deps image (also a deps-image tag input) |
| `DEPS_IMAGE_FILE` | Where `container.publish` records the resolved image reference |
| `PYSCN_REPORTS_DIR` | `reports/`, where pyscn writes its HTML and JSON reports |
| `UV_PUBLISH_SETTINGS` | The three `UV_PUBLISH_*` credentials; all set means the upload uses them, without attestations |
| `OIDC_ENV_VARS` | The OIDC token(s) the host injects into the publish job; present means Trusted Publishing. {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}`ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL`/`_TOKEN`{% else %}`PYPI_ID_TOKEN`{% endif %} on this host |
| `DIST_DIR` | `dist/`, where builds land and `release.attest` looks for wheels and sdists |
| `PYLOCK_FILE` | `reports/pylock.toml`, written by `develop.pylock` |
| `CHANGELOG_FILE` | `docs/reference/changelog.md`, which `release.changelog` updates and commits; must match `[tool.commitizen] changelog_file` |
| `SENTINEL` | Bootstrap sentinel file path |

`LOCAL_MODULE`, the path to `_CI/tasks/local.py`, lives in `__init__.py` rather than here: it is
the one constant the namespace wiring itself needs.

## The deps image and its cache

### It runs as a non-root user

`Dockerfile.deps` ends with `USER 1001`, so nothing in CI runs as root. Two things have
to line up for that to work, and both are in the Dockerfile:

- **`/app` is chowned to 1001.** The image is built with `--no-install-project`, so
  `uv run` installs the project into `UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT` (`/app/.venv`) at job
  time. Left root-owned, every task would fail on the first write.
- **The user has a home directory.** uv caches under `$HOME`.

**The uid is not arbitrary.** 1001 is the uid {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}GitHub's hosted runners{% else %}GitLab's runners commonly{% endif %} own the
mounted workspace with. If your runner uses a different uid, the workspace arrives
unwritable and jobs fail with permission errors on paths under the checkout — change the
uid in `Dockerfile.deps` to match. That edit changes the deps-image tag automatically,
because the Dockerfile is one of the tag's inputs, so you will not be served a stale
image built for the old uid.
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}

Worth checking on GitLab specifically: the clone is performed by a helper container, so
depending on runner configuration the project directory can arrive owned by root. If the
first job fails writing into the checkout, that mismatch is the likely cause.
{%- endif %}

CI jobs run inside a prebuilt image holding the project's dependencies, so each job
starts with a warm environment instead of resolving from scratch. `container.publish`
builds and publishes it; `.deps-image` records the reference downstream jobs consume.

**The tag is content-addressed.** `deps_image_tag()` hashes the lockfile, `Dockerfile.deps`
and the resolved base-image string together (each input reduced to a fixed-width digest
first, so no combination of inputs can collide with another). Change any one of them and you get a new tag — which
is what stops CI from silently reusing an image built from a stale Dockerfile or base.

**The reference is digest-pinned.** `container.publish` resolves the published image to
`repository@sha256:…` via the local repo digest{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %} (the pipeline's kaniko job reads kaniko's `--digest-file` instead){% endif %} rather than handing
downstream jobs a mutable tag. A tag repointed between the build job and a consumer job
therefore cannot change the container that consumer runs in.
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}

**Residual risk — reuse of an existing tag.** When the tag already exists in the registry,
the build is skipped and that image is reused. This keeps CI fast, but it trusts whoever
pushed the tag first: an actor able to pre-seed `<repo>-deps:<tag>` would have later jobs
run inside their image, including the publish job that holds PyPI OIDC. Digest pinning does not remove this — you would pin their digest.

Because the mitigation is an access-control one, keep it that way:

- Restrict write access to the `<repo>-deps` package to this pipeline. Only
  `build-deps-image` needs `packages: write`; every other job runs at `packages: read`.
- Audit who else can publish packages in the org, and treat that set as trusted to the
  same degree as the release credentials.
- To remove the trade-off entirely, drop the `manifest inspect` skip in
  `publish_deps_image()` so every run rebuilds from the repository. The cost is a full
  image build per gated run.
{%- else %}

**No reuse path.** Every run builds from the repository — kaniko in the pipeline job, docker
or podman from `container.publish` by hand — and there is no skip-if-the-tag-exists branch, so
the image can only ever be what that run produced.
{%- endif %}

## The publish pipeline

Every push to `main` runs one job, and the job publishes when the push carried a release: a
change of `[project].version` between the previous tip and the new one. `release` creates no
tag; `release.ship` creates it on the new tip of `main` through the host's Release API, once
the release has been reviewed and merged, so the merge method (merge commit, squash, rebase)
does not matter. The job then builds once and uploads exactly what it built. With no version
bump it prints so and exits 0.{% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %} `pages.yaml` asks the same
question through `release.detect` before deploying the docs.{% endif %}

1. `release.tag` — `v<version>` on the new tip, with a Release entry.
2. `release.dist` — clean, audit, build, and run the distribution checks.
3. `release.attest` — a PEP 740 attestation per wheel and sdist, `<file>.publish.attestation`{% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %},
   signed with the job's `SIGSTORE_ID_TOKEN`{% endif %}.
4. the upload — `uv publish` {% if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}exchanges `PYPI_ID_TOKEN` for a PyPI token and {% endif %}sends the files and their attestations.
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
5. `actions/attest-build-provenance` — GitHub's provenance attestation over the uploaded wheel and sdist.
{%- endif %}

Nothing rebuilds between the steps, so every attestation refers to a file that is uploaded.
[Verify a release attestation](../how-to/verify-a-release-attestation.md) checks the result;
[Set up Trusted Publishing](../how-to/set-up-trusted-publishing.md) covers the PyPI side.

## Bootstrap framework (`_CI/tasks/bootstrap.py`)

A sentinel file (`_CI/.bootstrapped`) ensures first-time setup runs exactly once; every task inherits bootstrap as an Invoke `pre` task. New setup steps are added by appending to the `STEPS` list:

```python
STEPS: list[BootstrapStep] = [
    BootstrapStep(
        name='pre-commit hooks',
        action=install_pre_commit,
        prompt='Install pre-commit hooks? [y/N] ',
        ci_behavior='skip',  # 'run' or 'skip' in CI
    ),
    # Add more steps here
]
```

Each step has a `prompt` for local interactive use and a `ci_behavior` for unattended execution — when `CI=true` is set, or stdin is not a terminal, prompts are suppressed and steps execute or skip accordingly. See [Customize the bootstrap](../how-to/customize-the-bootstrap.md) for the recipe.

## Directory layout

```
_CI/
  README.md            Pointer to these pages
  pyproject.toml       Ruff config for this tree and the vendored extra
  info.py              Stdlib-only reader of the pyproject values the deps image and bump-uv need
  uv_release.py        Stdlib-only uv release resolver behind develop.bump-uv
  tasks/
    __init__.py        Namespace aggregation + bootstrap wiring
    configuration.py   Centralized constants
    shared.py          Core decorators and utilities
    bootstrap.py       One-time setup framework
    build.py           Package build + twine and check-wheel-contents checks
    container.py       Docker image
    develop.py         Pre-commit management, uv pin, pylock export
    document.py        ProperDocs documentation
    format_.py         Ruff formatting + import sorting
    lint.py            Ruff, validate-pyproject, pylint, ty, complexipy, commitizen
    preflight.py       The step registry: every check, its scope, and its tier
    quality.py         pyscn analysis
    release.py         Version bump, changelog, PEP 740 attestations, publish
    sbom.py            CycloneDX SBOM composition
    secure.py          pip-audit, SBOM extract and validate
    test.py            pytest, the tox matrix, coverage, the coverage badge and ratchet
    {{ git_hosting_service }}.py          Host-specific helpers (registry, release PR/MR, Release API, pipeline badge, SBOM components)
    local.py           YOUR tasks — never overwritten by `copier update`
  lib/
    vendor/            Vendored Invoke (committed — don't hand-edit)
    vendor.txt         Pinned dependency list
    patches/           The two hand-maintained edits to the vendored tree
```

Everything in that tree is template-owned and replaced on `copier update` — **except
`local.py`**. It is listed in the template's `_skip_if_exists`, so it is seeded once and then
left alone. Put project-specific tasks there and updates stay conflict-free; put them in any
other module and that hunk needs resolving on every update. `__init__.py` includes it
automatically when present, with the same bootstrap pre-task the built-in namespaces get, so
there is no registration step. See [Add a workflow task](../how-to/add-a-workflow-task.md).

